# Operation Modes

Choose the appropriate mode based on your traffic patterns. Each mode is tuned for specific operational requirements.

## Scan Mode

{% card title="Scan Mode" icon="scan" description="Optimized for network discovery and enumeration tools like nmap, masscan, and port scanners." %}

`--scan-mode`

**Use Cases**

- Network mapping and reconnaissance
- Port scanning across multiple subnets
- Service enumeration
- Vulnerability scanning

**Characteristics**

- Low-latency packet forwarding
- High throughput for many connections
- Minimal buffering
- Suitable for UDP-heavy tools

{% /card %}

## Session Mode

{% card title="--session-mode" icon="session" description="Optimized for interactive sessions and persistent connections like SSH, RDP, and database connections." %}

**Use Cases**

- SSH terminal sessions
- Remote Desktop Protocol (RDP)
- Database connections (MySQL, PostgreSQL)
- Long-lived TCP sessions

**Characteristics**

- Connection stability prioritized
- Automatic reconnection handling
- Keepalive packet management
- Lower throughput, higher reliability

{% /card %}

### Examples

Network scanning through a pivot:

{% codeblock code="wallhack entry --listen :443 --scan-mode" title="Entry node optimized for nmap" /%}

Interactive SSH session through a pivot:

{% codeblock code="wallhack entry --listen :443 --session-mode" title="Entry node optimized for SSH/RDP" /%}

{% callout type="note" %}
If no mode is specified, wallhack uses balanced defaults suitable for general use. Specify a mode when you know your traffic patterns for optimal performance.
{% /callout %}

{% separator /%}

# Management Interfaces

wallhack provides both interactive and programmatic interfaces for monitoring and managing your tunnel infrastructure.

## REPL Interface

{% card title="REPL Interface" icon="repl" description="Interactive REPL for real-time tunnel management and monitoring." %}

`--repl`

**Features**

- View active connections and their status
- List connected peers and their roles
- Monitor traffic statistics per tunnel
- Execute commands to reconfigure routes
- Debug connection issues interactively

{% /card %}

## REST API

{% card title="REST API" icon="api" description="HTTP REST API for programmatic control and monitoring of tunnel infrastructure." %}

`--api :PORT`

**Features**

- Query connection status and metrics
- List all peers in the tunnel chain
- Add or remove routes dynamically
- Retrieve performance statistics
- Integrate with automation tools

{% /card %}

### Usage Examples

Launch wallhack with REPL interface:

{% codeblock code="wallhack entry --listen :443 --repl" title="Entry node with interactive REPL" /%}

Enable REST API on port 8080:

{% codeblock code="wallhack entry --listen :443 --api :8080" title="Entry node with REST API" /%}

Use both interfaces simultaneously:

{% codeblock code="wallhack entry --listen :443 --repl --api :8080" title="Entry node with REPL and REST API" /%}

{% callout type="note" %}
**Example REPL commands:** `peers` to list connected peers, `connections` to view active tunnels, `stats` to display traffic statistics.
{% /callout %}
